00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:49.810
Good morning, Mr. Splendor.

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:51.950
At the top of the morning to you, Captain Fairfield.

00:15:52.270 --> 00:15:53.670
Have you had any words yet from Blake?

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:54.290
Not yet.

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:56.550
You know my orders not to disclose our position

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:57.690
until we reach 30 South.

00:15:57.870 --> 00:15:59.190
Oh, sure, sure, sure.

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:01.780
Come in.

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:04.770
Oh, hello, Mr. Barzak.

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:06.010
What can I do for you?

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:09.410
I must send a message, and your radio operator refuses to transmit it.

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:10.670
I'm sorry, Mr. Barzak.

00:16:10.690 --> 00:16:11.530
He's following orders.

00:16:11.860 --> 00:16:15.220
We're using our radio only for ship's business from here until we reach Tangita.

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:16.760
But this message is important.

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:20.280
Sure it is more important that our workers and their families reach Tangita.

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:20.680
Right.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:24.140
That's why we're taking no chances on having the Baratavia's position known.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:25.380
Okay, Fairfield.

00:16:25.850 --> 00:16:26.370
Here they are, Captain.

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:34.840
And let that be a lesson to you, Mr. Barsak.

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:41.530
Marco, what about that code message for Barsak?

00:16:41.650 --> 00:16:43.010
I've just finished it, M-22.

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:43.570
Good.

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:46.800
Use a regular commercial band and send it to him on the Baratavia at once.

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:47.640
Yes, sir.

00:17:20.350 --> 00:17:24.890
Splendor. We've just contacted Tajida by radio. Your friend, Commander Winslow, is there.

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:30.000
That's fine. I had a hunch he'd be showing up there sooner or later after Blake wired me about the troubling

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:30.430
than having.

